+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

This port runs 64-bit Windows applications on OpenBSD/amd64. It does not
provide 32-bit Windows support. You can identify 32-bit binaries with
file(1) or when seeing syswow64 error like this:

wine: failed to load L"\\??\\C:\\windows\\syswow64\\ntdll.dll" error[...]

In that case, the program *can't* run.

Common Problems
===============

The first step if an application doesn't run should be to check

	https://appdb.winehq.org/

for an entry. Also make sure, no 32-bit binary or library is involved (see
above).

Many games need higher than default datasize (and potentially other)
limits, see ksh(1) for ulimit.

In case of audio issues, make sure to consult the pulseaudio(1) man page.

Many games expect that the executable is run from its directory and not
a different location. So for path/to/program.exe, make sure to:

	$ cd path/to/
	$ wine program.exe

Direct3D Translation Layers
===========================

${PKGSTEM}'s default Direct3D graphics support (Damawand) uses OpenGL.
If Vulkan is installed and supported by your hardware, you can try
alternative (faster) translation layers. DXVK for D3D8, 9, 10, and 11:

	https://github.com/doitsujin/dxvk

and/or VKD3D-Proton for D3D12:

	https://github.com/HansKristian-Work/vkd3d-proton

Both come with installation instructions and/or install scripts that
install the dll's into the wine prefix and update winecfg.

Steam
=====

The package installs wine-steam. This helper prepares Steam for this
64-bit-only port: it installs the client directly from Valve's update
packages, without running the Windows installer, and applies the prefix,
font and resource-limit settings described below.

As your normal desktop user, start PulseAudio as described above, then run:

    wine-steam

On the first run, it creates $HOME/.wine-steam and initializes Wine without
installing Mono or Gecko. It downloads the Windows 64-bit client packages
listed in Valve's manifest, verifies their SHA-256 checksums and extracts
them into:

    $HOME/.wine-steam/drive_c/Steam

An Internet connection is required for installation. No separate wineboot
command is needed. The helper always uses this dedicated prefix, separate
from the default $HOME/.wine prefix.

At each launch, it registers Liberation replacements for Arial, Times New
Roman and Courier New, raises the data-segment and open-file limits to the
user's existing hard limits, then starts Steam with -cef-disable-sandbox
(disabling the CEF browser sandbox).

Run the same command to start Steam again. If installation was interrupted,
run it again to retry. Additional command-line arguments are passed to Steam.

Debugging
=========

See WINEDEBUG in wine(1).
